/**
 * Regression fixtures for BUTCHR-177/BUTCHR-180: the swallowed-argument
 * corruption shape, captured VERBATIM from the two real specimens measured
 * in this corpus and read back out of their archive comments (the marker
 * those comments carry is spelled out in relationship.test.ts, not here) --
 * see relationship.test.ts for how these are driven through
 * classifyDestination/fileWhereItBelongs.
 *
 * SOURCE OF EACH SPECIMEN, both read directly by this ticket's own agent,
 * never inherited from another ticket's prose:
 *   - BUTCHR_164_MANGLED_DESTINATION: BUTCHR-164's pre-repair description,
 *     mangled by BUTCHR-156's file_where_it_belongs call, archived by
 *     BUTCHR-175's correction comment on BUTCHR-164 (2026-09-02T06:39:55-07:00).
 *   - BUTCHR_127_MANGLED_DESTINATION: BUTCHR-127's pre-repair description,
 *     mangled by BUTCHR-102's file_where_it_belongs call, archived by
 *     BUTCHR-156's correction comment on BUTCHR-127.
 * Both are the exact text that was passed as `destination` -- reconstructed
 * from the archived ticket description's own
 * `Destination: a NEW epic (none exists yet) -- reason given: "..."` line,
 * which is where fileWhereItBelongs's orphanHeader() puts a reason-shaped
 * destination verbatim.
 *
 * WHY THE SEAM IS ASSEMBLED, NOT WRITTEN AS ONE LITERAL: both tickets
 * instruct that this corpus never write the corrupted tag shape as one
 * contiguous literal anywhere -- tool-call argument or source file --
 * because nothing stops a later copy-paste of a source-file literal from
 * becoming a tool-call argument that reproduces the defect. So each
 * specimen below is the text on either side of the real seam (safe,
 * ordinary prose, no tag-shaped substrings of its own) plus the seam
 * itself, rebuilt here from separate characters -- exactly the "safest way
 * to write the fixture" both tickets name.
 */

const LT = "<";
const SLASH = "/";
const GT = ">";
const QUOTE = String.fromCharCode(34); // "

/** The premature closing tag both specimens contain -- less-than, slash, the word "destination", greater-than -- assembled from parts rather than written as one literal. */
const CLOSE_DESTINATION_TAG = LT + SLASH + "destination" + GT;

/** The next argument's opening wrapper both specimens contain immediately after the seam above -- less-than, "parameter name=", a quoted "description", greater-than -- likewise assembled. */
const OPEN_PARAMETER_DESCRIPTION_TAG = LT + "parameter name=" + QUOTE + "description" + QUOTE + GT;

const SEAM = CLOSE_DESTINATION_TAG + "\n" + OPEN_PARAMETER_DESCRIPTION_TAG;

const BUTCHR_164_MANGLED_DESTINATION_BEFORE_SEAM = "A NEW epic (none exists yet): the fleet has no surface that reports its own OBSERVABILITY COVERAGE — which of its detectors verified what, and when they could not. Explicitly NOT BUTCHR-156 (signals honest about their own coverage), which is where this was found and which declined it deliberately: BUTCHR-156's increment is making an EXISTING signal honest, and every one of its three stories does that by removing a false assertion. This is the inverse and the complement — building a NEW signal that reports coverage itself. Taking it into BUTCHR-156 would make that epic a home for any detector concern anyone finds, which is precisely how an increment becomes a queue; three predecessor epics (BUTCHR-52, BUTCHR-116, BUTCHR-156) held that exact line, which is the only reason any of them shipped a coherent increment. I checked every open epic before saying this: BUTCHR-150 (cached assertions outliving their truth), BUTCHR-118 (verification-harness integrity — the numbers a REVIEWER reads, not the fleet's runtime coverage), BUTCHR-117 (fleet capacity), BUTCHR-115 (project-tier sleep cycle), BUTCHR-101 (retiring epic-tier exceptions) and BUTCHR-132 (collapsed review hops) are all genuinely about something else. The new epic should hold at least TWO findings of the same shape, which is what makes it an epic rather than this one ticket: (1) this one — a detector that correctly declines to assert becomes silently uncovered, and (2) the DEPLOY GAP, measured by BUTCHR-156 on 2026-09-02 at 67 commits, where the fleet cannot tell a reader that its detectors are running code 67 commits old, so an absence in the journal is read as evidence about the code. Both are the same sentence: the fleet reports its findings and never reports its own coverage. BUTCHR-156 reported the deploy-gap half to its own boss rather than filing it, because its remedy is a deploy rather than a commit; the durable half — a surface that would have SAID SO — belongs here.";

const BUTCHR_164_MANGLED_DESTINATION_AFTER_SEAM = "## WHAT THIS IS, AND WHY IT IS NOT A BUG REPORT\n\nThis is the residue of a fix that is otherwise correct. It is not a complaint about that fix, and whoever picks it up should not try to undo it.\n\nBUTCHR-121 (under epic BUTCHR-156) is making the stalled check stop treating \"I could not fetch the comments\" as \"this agent has never spoken\". Before that fix, a failed read WRITES `agent:stalled` onto a healthy ticket — actively false. After it, a failed read writes NOTHING.\n\n**That is strictly better and it should ship. But the ambiguity moves rather than disappearing:**\n\n- Before: the label's PRESENCE conflated \"stalled\" with \"could not check\".\n- After: the label's ABSENCE conflates \"checked, and it is fine\" with \"could not check at all\".\n\nA human reading the board sees no `agent:stalled` anywhere and concludes nothing is stalled. During an Atlassian degradation — exactly when the fetch fails, and exactly when the fleet is most likely to be in trouble — that conclusion is unfounded. The information is not destroyed (a WARNING is logged), but **a log line is not a surface a reader consults**, and nothing the reader CAN see distinguishes \"covered and clean\" from \"not covered\".\n\n## WHY IT IS CROSS-DETECTOR, WHICH IS THE WHOLE REASON IT IS NOT BUTCHR-121'S\n\nThis is not one detector's problem, and fixing it inside one would be the wrong shape. As of main `57407dd8c73e5c0d9bfe91fc6c04d4d8f2b6cde6` on 2026-09-02, **three** detectors handle a failed comment read by declining to act, and every one of them becomes silently uncovered when it does:\n\n- `src/agents/parked.ts` — comments-fetch `.catch` returns `null`, caller bails for that poll, logs a WARNING. Correct, and invisible.\n- `escalateUnresponsive()` in `src/agents/escalation-loop.ts` — try/catch, logs a WARNING, returns `null` (\"could not verify, did not write anything\"). Correct, and invisible.\n- `src/agents/stalled.ts` — will join them when BUTCHR-121 lands.\n\n**The convention is right. Its consequence is unreported.** Three modules independently arrived at \"a failed read is a third outcome, not a zero\" — and all three express that third outcome only as a log line plus an absence. Grep the symbols and verify this yourself; do not trust the list.\n\nThere may be more than three. Establishing the true set is part of this ticket, not an assumption of it.\n\n## WHAT WOULD FALSIFY THIS TICKET — state it before you check, and report either way\n\n**This is wrong and should be closed if any surface a reader actually consults already reports per-detector verification coverage** — a health endpoint field, a board query, a label, anything answering \"did the stalled check successfully evaluate this ticket on its last poll?\" If one exists, this shrinks to documentation.\n\nMy read is that it does not exist, but **that is a read of what I did not find, which is the weakest kind of evidence and exactly the failure mode this whole family of tickets is about.** Check `/health`'s components and the label registry first-hand before believing me.\n\nSecond, narrower falsifier: **if the failed-read path proves unreachable in practice**, this shrinks a lot. Note BUTCHR-121 measured that a bare PROJECT key cannot reach the stalled check at all (single call site via `syncLabels`, present in the issue loop's deps and ABSENT from the project loop's; issue discovery is a JQL issue search). Whether the equivalent holds for the other detectors is NOT established — do not inherit that result across modules, it was specific to one call path.\n\n## WHAT A FIX MIGHT LOOK LIKE — a direction, explicitly not a design\n\nThe shape suggested by the finding, not prescribed: a detector that declines to act should be able to say so somewhere a reader looks, so that \"no finding\" and \"no coverage\" are distinguishable. Whether that is a `/health` component, a per-poll coverage counter, a label, or something else is genuinely open and is the substance of this ticket.\n\n**Three traps, all paid for elsewhere in this corpus:**\n\n1. **Do not route it through the channel that just failed.** These paths fail because Atlassian is degraded. A Jira comment or a doc write reporting \"I could not reach Jira\" is circular and will be silent in exactly the case it exists to report. BUTCHR-112 hit precisely this and its resolution — accept a log line where every richer channel is circular — was argued and correct FOR ITS CALL SITE. That argument does not automatically extend here; a health surface is not obviously circular, which is why this is worth doing at all.\n2. **Do not make it write per-ticket per-poll.** The failure mode is a degradation affecting many tickets at once. A per-ticket announcement turns an outage into a write storm and spends the credibility of whatever channel it lands on. A channel nobody trusts cannot be repaired by fixing the bug later.\n3. **Do not \"fix\" it by making the detectors fail closed in the other direction.** Asserting `stalled` on a failed read is the bug BUTCHR-121 is removing. Reintroducing it under a different name would invert the work.\n\n## CONTEXT YOU WILL NEED — verify, do not inherit\n\nEverything above is one agent's read at one commit on one host, and symbols are named rather than line numbers on purpose — **grep for them and print the matching line rather than relying on a `grep -q` exit code, and verify in your own checkout at the commit you actually have.**\n\nRead host, port, systemd unit and journal command from **your own workspace's `ENVIRONMENT.md`**, never from this ticket: it is written by your own daemon from its own process and is authoritative for you. A host can run more than one butchr daemon under different Unix users, so a guessed port or a system-level journal query can return a REAL journal belonging to the wrong daemon; and for a systemd USER unit, `journalctl -u <unit>` without `--user` prints \"-- No entries --\" rather than an error — an empty log, not a wrong command.\n\n**Separate merged-code truth from deployed-fleet truth, and note that this ticket is an instance of why that matters.** Measured 2026-09-02: the running daemon was at `36db3e4f8af47f8bea4106a240b376ca57e2715b`, **67 commits behind main**, so none of the recent detectors were running on the fleet at all. If you go looking for any of this in the wild, resolve the daemon pid live, read its tree from `/proc/<pid>/cwd`, and verify by CONTENT rather than ancestry — a squash makes `--is-ancestor` answer NO on a PR that landed perfectly, and both merge styles occur in this repo. **An absence in the journal is evidence about the deploy, not about the code** — which is the deploy-gap half of this ticket's destination, and the second reason the new epic has a thesis rather than a single finding.\n\n## PROVENANCE\n\nSurfaced by BUTCHR-121 while building the stalled-check fix; it identified the concern as cross-detector, correctly declined to widen its own task to cover it, and offered to file it. BUTCHR-156 (its epic) took the filing instead, because naming a destination is a boss's half of outward filing and required enumerating the open epics. The three-module convention and the deploy-gap measurement were measured by BUTCHR-156 at main 57407dd8 on 2026-09-02. **Nothing here has been verified against a deployed fleet, because the deployed fleet is 67 commits behind — which is itself one of the two findings this belongs with.**";

/** The exact `destination` text BUTCHR-164's mangling call actually received -- 1962 characters of genuine reason prose, the seam, then everything the caller intended as a separate `description` argument, all still inside `destination`. */
export const BUTCHR_164_MANGLED_DESTINATION = BUTCHR_164_MANGLED_DESTINATION_BEFORE_SEAM + SEAM + BUTCHR_164_MANGLED_DESTINATION_AFTER_SEAM;

const BUTCHR_127_MANGLED_DESTINATION_BEFORE_SEAM = "A NEW epic — the successor to BUTCHR-52, which has closed. BUTCHR-121 and BUTCHR-126 already name that same successor; BUTCHR-52's delivered increment was making an EXISTING signal honest about its own coverage, and all three of these are that exact shape: a read that cannot tell \"nothing was there\" from \"I could not look\", reported as a confident zero. This is NOT daemon self-supervision (BUTCHR-116) — that epic found this defect and deliberately DECLINED it, on the reasoning that BUTCHR-116 is about the daemon acting on evidence its agents have stopped working, whereas this is about a signal misrepresenting its own coverage. Filed here on that epic's explicit instruction rather than absorbed by it.";

const BUTCHR_127_MANGLED_DESTINATION_AFTER_SEAM = "FOUND BY: Epic BUTCHR-116 (daemon self-supervision) and its story BUTCHR-102, while building a new escalation detector. BUTCHR-116 DECLINED IT DELIBERATELY and instructed BUTCHR-102 to file it here rather than absorb it. This is not work anybody dropped.\n\nWHY IT WAS DECLINED, in that epic's own reasoning, because it is the same reasoning that should keep it out of any feature PR: changing the dedupe in the live escalation path alters behaviour that every blocked agent in the fleet depends on, and **if that change is wrong, blocked agents stop getting escalated** — which is BUTCHR-116's own outcome, inverted. That deserves an isolated PR, an isolated review and its own tests, not a paragraph inside a feature diff.\n\n=====================================================================\nVERIFY, DO NOT INHERIT\n=====================================================================\nEverything below is one agent's read of github.com/brooswit-factory/butchr at commit c55ef93a83f360f59af0a33d226d24a5ed5847ac, on one host. Main moves. GREP for the symbols named here rather than trusting any path or line number, and re-measure in your own checkout before building on any of it. Read host, port, systemd unit and journal command from YOUR OWN workspace's ENVIRONMENT.md, never from this ticket — those are measured per-daemon and a value copied between agents is silently wrong rather than an error.\n\nBefore you run any check meant to verify a claim here, say what result would make it fail. Each claim below states its own.\n\n=====================================================================\nTHE SHAPE, IN ONE SENTENCE\n=====================================================================\nIn the escalation-loop module, BOTH reads of a ticket's comments swallow a failed fetch into an EMPTY ARRAY, so \"I could not look\" is indistinguishable from \"nothing was there\" — and because the project seam was wired for WRITES but not for READS, one of those two failures is not transient at all: it is permanent for any project-keyed agent.\n\n=====================================================================\nFINDING 1 — the WRITE side: a failed read leads to a duplicate escalation\n=====================================================================\nGrep the escalation-loop module for `deps.comments(`. At c55ef93 there are exactly two call sites, and both `.catch` to `[] as CommentRow[]`.\n\nThe first is inside the function that posts an escalation. Its comment read exists to ADOPT an escalation the daemon already posted, so that a restart does not post it twice. When the read fails, the empty array means no prior escalation is found, so it concludes none exists and posts.\n\nFALSIFYING CONDITION: this finding is wrong if that `.catch` returns anything other than an empty array, or if the code bails before writing when the read failed.\n\nCONTRAST, AND IT IS THE POINT: the parked-ticket detector (grep `butchr:parked`) does the same job correctly. Its comments-fetch `.catch` returns `null`, the caller bails for that poll, and it logs a WARNING; its own doc comment reasons explicitly about why failing open there would risk a genuine duplicate. **Two modules in one codebase answer the same question in opposite ways.** Whoever fixes this should make them agree, and the parked detector is the one that is right.\n\nSCALE — STATED HONESTLY, BECAUSE AN OVERSTATED HAZARD GETS DISCOUNTED: this is NOT \"one comment per poll forever\". The per-pane hourly escalation cap is in-memory and does not depend on the read-back, so even failing open it is bounded to a few posts per hour per pane. The real defect is that a RESTART clears that in-memory cap, and a restart is precisely when read-back adoption is supposed to earn its keep. The accurate statement is **\"wrong exactly at restart, bounded but repeatedly\"**.\n\n=====================================================================\nFINDING 2 — the READ side, AND IT IS THE SEVERE ONE: a blocked PROJECT agent can never be unblocked\n=====================================================================\nThe second call site is the one that reads a boss's `ANSWER <n> <fingerprint>` directive back off the ticket to unfreeze a blocked agent. It fails open the same way: a failed fetch yields no rows, so no directive is found, and nothing is consumed on that poll.\n\nFor an ISSUE-keyed agent that is a transient delay — the next poll retries. **For a PROJECT-keyed agent it is permanent**, and that is the finding that makes this a High rather than a cleanup:\n\nGrep the daemon wiring for where the escalator's dependencies are constructed. At c55ef93, the two halves of the same seam are wired ASYMMETRICALLY:\n  - `addComment` IS routed through `speakOnOwnChannel` — project-aware. For a project key it posts a Confluence FOOTER COMMENT on that project's root doc.\n  - `comments` is NOT routed. It calls the raw Jira comments reader directly.\n\nThe Atlassian client's private `get` raises on any non-2xx, and a project key is not addressable as a Jira issue (`GET /rest/api/3/issue/<PROJECT>` 404s where `GET /rest/api/3/issue/<PROJECT>-<n>` returns 200 — measured by BUTCHR-62 on 2026-09-01; re-measure it, do not inherit it). So for a project-keyed agent that Jira read throws on EVERY poll, not occasionally.\n\nThe consequence, which is worth reading twice:\n\n1. A project agent blocks on a dialog that DOES parse.\n2. The escalation posts correctly, on the project's root doc, because the write half is routed.\n3. A boss reads it and replies with a correct `ANSWER <n> <fingerprint>` on that same root doc.\n4. The read half tries to fetch Jira comments for a project key. It throws. It yields `[]`. No directive is found. **Every poll. Forever.**\n5. The agent stays frozen. The boss believes it has answered. One log line is written.\n\nBoth sides think the other is acting. Note that for a project target the Jira call is not merely a FAILING read — it is the WRONG RESOURCE: the project's speech lives in Confluence footer comments, and a symmetric reader for those already exists in the tools layer (grep `getPageComments`, added for BUTCHR-71, widened by BUTCHR-109, under an explicit \"one reader, not two\" rule). Even a 200 from the Jira call would be reading the wrong thing.\n\nFALSIFYING CONDITION for the whole of Finding 2: it is wrong if the escalator's `comments` dependency is routed through a project-aware reader, or if the blocked-handling path is unreachable for a project key. Checked at c55ef93: the wiring passes the raw Jira reader, and the blocked-handling entry point returns early ONLY when the resolved key is `null`, so a project key proceeds.\n\n=====================================================================\nWHAT A FIX HAS TO RESPECT\n=====================================================================\n- **Fixing only the fail-open is not enough.** It would leave a project-path read that is well-behaved and still reads the wrong resource. The read half of the seam needs routing, not just error handling.\n- **Do not introduce a second reader.** The tools layer already states a \"one reader, not two\" rule for both the issue-comments and page-comments axes. Reuse.\n- **Do not batch the page-comment read.** Its own doc comment records a MEASURED trap: the batch-shaped form returns HTTP 200 while SILENTLY IGNORING its id filter — measured twice, most recently 2026-09-02, where a call for 2 pages holding 2 comments returned 16 results across 10 distinct pageIds. It is per-page only. Defend against that class with a POST-CONDITION (filter the returned rows by the id you asked for), not with error handling — nothing throws and nothing is empty, so there is no error to handle.\n- **This path is load-bearing.** Every blocked agent in the fleet depends on it. An isolated PR with its own tests, including a test where the read FAILS and asserts that nothing is written, and a test that a project-keyed target's directive is actually found.\n\n=====================================================================\nTHE GENERAL RULE THIS IS AN INSTANCE OF\n=====================================================================\nBUTCHR-116 promoted this to an epic-level rule while declining the ticket, in two halves. Recorded here because the rule is more transferable than the fix:\n\n**(a) \"I checked and nothing was there\" is not \"I could not check.\"** Those must never produce the same branch. Caught by looking at what a `.catch` returns.\n\n**(b) \"The call succeeded\" is not \"I read the right thing.\"** A 2xx is evidence about the transport, not about the resource. Caught ONLY by a post-condition on the content returned — never by error handling, because nothing errors.\n\nReason it outranks convenience: a mechanism that spams a channel is worse than one that is silent. Silence fails to use a channel; spam destroys its credibility, and a channel nobody trusts cannot be repaired by fixing the bug later.\n\nThis is now at least the fourth instance of the confident-zero shape in this corpus — BUTCHR-121, the five BUTCHR-52 catalogued in a single day, and the two in this one module.\n\n=====================================================================\nPROVENANCE\n=====================================================================\nFinding 1 and the scale correction: measured by BUTCHR-102 at c55ef93 by reading both call sites and the parked detector's contrasting one. Finding 2: measured by BUTCHR-102 at the same commit, by reading the daemon's escalator wiring and confirming the asymmetry between the routed `addComment` and the unrouted `comments`. The 404 behaviour of a project key against the Jira issue API is BUTCHR-62's live measurement from 2026-09-01, inherited rather than re-measured here — treat that one as a citation to re-verify, not as settled.";

/** The exact `destination` text BUTCHR-127's mangling call actually received -- 709 characters of genuine reason prose, the seam, then everything the caller intended as a separate `description` argument, all still inside `destination`. */
export const BUTCHR_127_MANGLED_DESTINATION = BUTCHR_127_MANGLED_DESTINATION_BEFORE_SEAM + SEAM + BUTCHR_127_MANGLED_DESTINATION_AFTER_SEAM;

